ly dictate what  your end users will use in terms of  let's say a pcr machine  so you need to make sure that you give  your end users  as wide a choice as possible  in terms of whatever that they already  have your  essay is able to work on their system  this  slide actually shows the difference  between  resolute 2.0 and a conventional pcr test  so resolute 2.0 is a direct pcr test  what that means is it  it is a pcr where you don't need to do  an additional step of sample prep so  there's  absolutely no ironing extraction you  take the specimen directly  from collection point and then you put  it in the pcr  master mix and then pcr  so that can so that is what resolute  is on the top of the slide um  so you go from a sample collection  whether it is  uh specifically for for resolute 2.0  it is applicable to whether it is nasal  soap whether it is a throat swab or  nasopharyngeal swab  so resolute 2.0 is a copic 19 rtpcr test  it's a direct tpcr test for kobit 19.  so if you start from the first box of  the workflow on the top  we are looking at a sample specimen that  are either nasal  nasopharyngeal or throat and  then of course a certain transport media  and we have tested resolute 2.0  in a number of a  very very specific brand and type of  collection media and it's all stated so  that the users  are aware which are the ones that have  been validated  so if they're not using any of the ones  that have been validated and listed  then the essay may or may not perform  as well as what we had stated and where  the dotted line is basically  is where the lab process starts  so at the lab basically what you do is  you just add five microliter of  the sample directly into a master mix  and  you start the pcr reaction and the whole  process  should not take more than one and a half  hours the pcr  itself actually takes 60 minutes so we  are saying that  you know the taking the five microliter  of the  the patient specimen into the pcr well  depending on how many you do  if you do the whole 96 well um it could  take about 15 minutes  so all the way from the sample arriving  to the lab  and operators starting to work on the  sample to the finish  of the pcr to the result the report  coming out  is maybe about one and a half um  probably not more than one and a half  hours  so that's the whole workflow of resolute  2.0  compare that with the bottom of the  slide a conventional  conventional rt-pcr basically the  workflow  before lab is the same you um  take the swap and then you use a certain  collection media  the difference actually is what happens  in the lab  when the when the sample arrives in the  lab for a conventional rt pcr  you actually need to run rna extraction  the rna extraction here we put down one  and a half hours  so the one and a half hours actually  refers to  labs that actually have a  an rna extraction system that would be  able to run  96 sample um uh  you know at the same time so this is  actually in the context of running 96 uh  well um pcr so you're trying to do a 96  well pcr  then if you are a lab that  is equipped with um  rna extraction that is able to do 96 in  a row  then yes it would take about one and a  half hours but if not  if you're using you know worse if you're  using a manual system  you would be doing that one at a time it  could take multiple  hours even some of the other  systems that are able to do higher  throughput um but not  96 what that means is in order for you  to run a 96 well  pcr run you would have to wait for the  rna extraction  to finish so if your rna extraction  cycle can only do 14 in a row you have  to wait about  i don't know seven hours before you can  batch  enough to run one plate  so what that means is in that in that  system  it would take you about nine hours to do  one plate of 96  versus one and a half hours to do the  same  plate of 96 just the lag process itself  there's a wide range for conventional  rtpcr of three to six  hours depending on the rna extraction  system you use  in the lab even if you have a 96  sample rna extraction system it will  take you  double the time of direct pcr like  resolute 2.0  at the end of the day we are talking  about the turnaround time  and turnaround time is not just  having an essay that can run a test  very quickly but it is also about having  an  essay that may not need to  be very very quick but you can do 96  in a row so all that actually  would be part of the consideration when  we're talking about how do we increase  the throughput  resolute 2.0 is actually a process of  iterative  improvement we started resolute 2.0  from resolute 1.0 so that's what  actually this slide is showing the  the progress of how we continue to  improve  as a result of um feedback from users  and then we come back from the end to  end so we we went to the end users  we came back and we iterate and we  improve and then we went back  uh with an improved product as a result  of that so if you look at the  progression  of what we have done using this what we  call accelerated productization process  um it's taken us really only from april  to july to roll out three different  uh format of the same test  so we started with the resolute 1.0  where it basically does very much  similar things but in a slightly  different format  so the essay is slightly different the  system  is slightly different and if you look at  resolute one it's got 14 tubes in terms  of the reagents  and of course we very very quickly came  back and with an improved version  you know through the iterative process  of resolute two  where it is now only four tubes also  made some  changes in the way that the essay was  done  added differences in the how the system  is done  and very very recently in july what we  have added was  automation um automation to basically  remember you remember the the chart  where we talked about  when the when the sample arrives in the  lab  somebody has to actually allocate the  sample into the pcr mix  so that part is still quite manual  that part somebody actually needs to  uncap the utm tube  allocate into the 96 well play and then  kept the utn tube  we actually now have an automation  solution  sample prep for resolute two literally  is allocating  so the automation allows us to do that  so we  now are able to cut down even on  the human errors so we don't need to  have someone that is  in full ppe standing in front of the  biosafety cabinet doing  aloe potting for many hours in a row so  this actually shows  um a very nice picture  of the process as a result of the  process that we have developed in dxd  hub  to be able to very quickly um  go through not just to make the first  product but also  to iterate and to make to continue to  make improvement to push our pipeline  product  in response to the feedback from the  customers  after introducing resolute 2.0 i have to  introduce the teams that are responsible  for developing resolute 2.0  it was developed together by three teams  mainly the teams at dso defense science  organization dxe hub and our colleague  at artc  a star so the aitc a star team  was responsible for developing the  automation  to run the the sample prep for resolute  2.0  and the dxo team was led by dr tanbun  hon  the exe head team was led by dr juan  rayford and the iartc a star team  was led by dr david lowe so i  want to take this opportunity to um  to maybe highlight the the  a very very fundamental element which i  didn't talk about throughout the whole  presentation  was the fact that for any of this to be  possible i always like to tell people  that  the success of a diagnostics test  really is dependent on how well you work  with your partners  because of the iterative process that's  required for you to keep  improving your products so that you are  able to keep  having a successful  market deployment and market adoption of  your test i hope that short  presentation has given you  some understanding or better  understanding of what it takes to build  a diagnostics test  thank you


